"rnbqkbnr/ppp1pppp/8/3p4/2PP4/8/PP2PPPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq -":{"variation":"slav","moves":[{"uci":"c7c6","book":true,"notes":[{"text":"These two moves define the Slav: solid, but developing your light bishop is hard and White can target your queenside…","original":"Slav Defense is defined by this 2 moves. Opening is very solid for black and it needs a lot of strategical and tactical understanding to play it right.\nThe biggest reason why so many players choose to play Slav Defense is because it offers a lot of possibilities and high chance of winning the games.\nBlack has few problems;\n1.) Developing his Queenside bishop ( LSB ) can be very difficult and if not developed, often going into loss.\n2.) Blacks pawn structure offers white a lot of targets. Especially so called \" Pawn minority attack\" [ White pawns: a3-b4 Blacks pawns: a6-b5-c6 ]\nI will make 3 main lines for Slav Defense:\n1.) Pure Slav ( Main line Slav )\n2.) Exchange variation\n3.) Semi-Slav ( the most complicated line )\n4.) Other responses such as 4. Qc2 or 4.Qb3\nI will avoid Chebachenko 4. .. a6 for black as I think it gives more options to white than to black. I avoid playing it as it is proved that after white plays 5.c5 has huge space advantage and Bf4 can keep the strong diagonal from h2-b8."}]}]},
"rnbqkbnr/pp2pppp/2p5/3p4/2PP4/8/PP2PPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq -":{"variation":"slav","moves":[{"uci":"g1f3","games":[297504,709764,1345840,2061668,2540906]},{"uci":"b1c3","notes":[{"text":"Nc3 stops Bf5, so your best reply is e6, transposing into the Semi-Slav.","original":"3. Nc3 does not let black play Bf5. As after pawn exchange via cxd4 cxd4 . Qb3 simply wins a pawn, for no compensation for black.\nOn Nc3, best move for black is 3. .. e6 and it transposes into Semi-Slav.\nIf late pawn exchange happens , like 4. cxd4 , black recaptures with 4. .. exd4","ideas":[{"type":"plan","side":"black","routes":[{"from":"e7","to":"e6","moves":1}]}]}],"games":[789570,2086522,4247065,6893258,8071279]},{"uci":"c4d5","games":[242357,543774,969755,1476434,1697158]}]},
"rnbqkbnr/pp2pppp/2p5/3p4/2PP4/5N2/PP2PPPP/RNBQKB1R b KQkq -":{"variation":"slav","moves":[{"uci":"g8f6","book":true}]},
"rnbqkb1r/pp2pppp/2p2n2/3p4/2PP4/5N2/PP2PPPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq -":{"variation":"slav","moves":[{"uci":"b1c3","games":[77078,207751,445954,856448,1501417]},{"uci":"d1c2","notes":[{"text":"White's queen eyes f5, so skip Bf5 and Bg4; play g6, dxc4 or e6 instead.","original":"Simmilar position can be achieved by playing Catalan. Main reason why white plays 4. Qc2 or 4.Qb3 , is to avoid natural development of LSB to either f4 or g4.\nWhite players who play Qc2, often fianchetto the LSB on g2 and wait to move e pawn for later in game.\nBlack has to play some other move instead of Bf5 or Bg4.\nRecommended and most played moves here are ;\n4. .. g6\n4. ..dxc4\n4. ..e6","ideas":[{"type":"purpose","from":"c2","relation":"controls","targets":["f5"]}]}],"games":[553,1682,5023,15135,51412]},{"uci":"d1b3","notes":[{"text":"Recommended: take on c4 when a queen or bishop can guard it, or play e6 for Semi-Slav positions.","original":"There are few ways to approach this position.\nI would recommend 4. .. dxc4 ( which I always try to play when stronger piece defends c4 pawn , such as Queen or Bishop ) or 4. .. e6 which can lead to Semi-Slav positions."}],"games":[201,534,1507,3644,12259]}]},
"rnbqkb1r/pp2pppp/2p2n2/3p4/2PP4/2N2N2/PP2PPPP/R1BQKB1R b KQkq -":{"variation":"slav","moves":[{"uci":"d5c4","book":true,"notes":[{"text":"This is the Pure Slav: you plan ...b5 to keep the extra pawn and give your light bishop room.","original":"This is so called \"Pure Slav\". The idea behind this is 5. ... b5 and black is keeping a pawn. Also, the blacks LSB now has 2 squares to go. That solves usual trouble black players have in Slav Defense.\nWhite has 4 options here for counterplay;\n1.) 5.e3 : I believe this is the most common move with the idea of retaking the c pawn and later on making the full center with Qe2-e4.\n2.) 5. a4 ; Immediatelly disabling black for playing b5. Black can develop his bishop to f5 or g4.\n3.) 5. Ne5 ; Probably the most dynamic move in this position. White immediatelly attacks c4 pawn. Black has a lot of counterplay here, either defending the c pawn or fast developing his LSB.\n4.) e4 ; Very aggressive countinuation for white. If black is not prepared, white can win very easy.","ideas":[{"type":"plan","side":"black","routes":[{"from":"b7","to":"b5","moves":1}]}]}],"games":[22074,47606,95712,200444,399069]},{"uci":"e7e6","book":true,"notes":[{"text":"This move defines the Semi-Slav: you threaten to take on c4 and defend it with b5.","original":"This is the move which defines Semi-Slav defense. Highly versatile opening. Black threatens to take on c4 and defend it with b5 which leads to very sharp games.\nBlack has trouble with developing his LSB, but the solid position can often compensate for it.\nMost common moves here are 5.e3 or 5.Bg5. Those moves occur in 70% of the games.\nThe less common moves are 5.g3 and 5.Qb3\nThis is very complex opening for both sides, so I will make 3 main chapters about Semi-Slav. For common moves there will be chapter for each, while the less common will be put in one.","ideas":[{"type":"plan","side":"black","routes":[{"from":"d5","to":"c4","moves":1},{"from":"b7","to":"b5","moves":1}]}]}],"games":[58592,158836,343380,687508,1236610]}]},
"rnbqkb1r/pp2pppp/2p2n2/8/2pP4/2N2N2/PP2PPPP/R1BQKB1R w KQkq -":{"variation":"pure-slav","moves":[{"uci":"e2e3","games":[8027,15835,25260,38682,54861]},{"uci":"a2a4","games":[1228,4932,18763,69740,204291]},{"uci":"f3e5","notes":[{"text":"This line is tricky: played carelessly, you can lose quickly, so know the theory well.","original":"Variation if not played right, black can lose in few moves. Very tricky and knowing the theory and system of this line is very important if you ever want to play \"Pure Slav\"."}],"games":[176,238,403,1070,4395]},{"uci":"e2e4","games":[18589,35690,61320,99463,137578]}]},
"rnbqkb1r/pp2pppp/2p2n2/8/2pP4/2N1PN2/PP3PPP/R1BQKB1R b KQkq -":{"variation":"pure-slav","moves":[{"uci":"b7b5","book":true,"notes":[{"text":"Your b5 pawn guards the c4 pawn, so White must fight to win it back.","original":"Black is not giving white easy chance to recapture the c pawn. White needs to put some fight in order to get it back.\nDefending c4 pawn in Queens Gambit is always risky.","ideas":[{"type":"purpose","from":"b5","relation":"defends","targets":["c4"]}]}]}]},
